    "content": " For travel to Europe in 2026, U.S. citizens must have a valid passport and, starting in late 2026, obtain an ETIAS authorization for Schengen Area countries. Key tips include traveling with light luggage for cobblestone streets, visiting between April-June or Sept-Nov to avoid peak crowds and heat, and utilizing trains for travel between major cities like London, Paris, and Rome. ETIAS authorization Key Planning Requirements Documentation: Passports must be valid for at least three months beyond your planned departure date. Entry Requirements (2026): The new European Travel Information and Authorisation System (ETIAS) is expected to launch in late 2026, requiring pre-registration for visa-exempt travelers. EES System: As of late 2025/2026, the Entry/Exit System (EES) will replace manual passport stamps with automated, biometric e-gates. Currency/Payments: While many countries use the Euro, some (like the UK, Switzerland, and Sweden) have their own currency. Credit cards are widely accepted. Top Destinations & Itinerary Tips Classic Capital Tour: London, Paris, Amsterdam, Berlin, Prague. Southern Europe: Barcelona, Lisbon, Rome, and the Amalfi Coast offer rich history and food. Hidden Gems & Coastal: Cyprus is recommended for its year-round good weather and beaches. Efficient Travel: Use high-speed trains for travel between countries and urban centers. Travel Tips Luggage: Pack light; you will likely walk long distances over uneven streets and stairs. Language: While English is common in tourist spots, learning a few local phrases is appreciated. Budgeting: Book accommodations and travel early to secure lower prices. Resources Flights: Compare options on Skyscanner or Google Flights. Skyscanner Google Flights Accommodation: Use Booking.com or Hostelworld. Booking.com Hostelworld Activities: Use GetYourGuide to book tours. GetYourGuide",
